<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zenon of Kaunos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 250s–230s BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Zeno</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zenon of Kaunos was a Greek official in Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> around the 250s–230s BCE, known from the Zenon Papyri, an archive of over 2,000 documents in Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script">Demotic</a> found near Kom el-Kharaba in the winter of 1914–1915. A native of Kaunos in Caria, he settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> and became private secretary to Apollonius, finance minister to Ptolemy II and Ptolemy III. His letters record economic life in Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, his travels through &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>&quot; in 258–259 BCE, exports of grain, oil and asphalt, and the purchase and shipment of enslaved boys and girls, some of whom escaped.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The papyri of <b>Zeno of Caunus</b>, from the same reign, mention Arabs between the Nile and the Red Sea as police officers and shepherds.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
